  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1244 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nToday: Heavy Rain Potential This Morning and Storm Potential for the \nAfternoon\n\nCurrent IR satellite view shows an area of convergence from just \nsouth of the Twin Cities metro over to near the Green Bay metro. \nStorms have begun to fire north of I-94 along this boundary. \nThis boundary should gradually shift south through the overnight\nhours. South of this boundary the low level jet continues to \npush 25 to 40kts of southwest flow into our CWA. PWATs remain \nquite high (1.8 to 2.1\") with instability values around 1500-\n2500 J/kg of MLCAPE. Limiting factor is 75-200 J/kg of MLCIN \nthrough the morning. With veering winds aloft through the \nmorning, in addition to the above parameters, training storms \nare possible (20 to 40%) which could lead to localized flooding,\nparticularly for areas east of the Mississippi River. Total \nrainfall amounts could be upwards of 1 to 3 inches with some \nlocations potentially seeing 3 to 5 inches as the 12Z HREF LPMM \nhas shown for areas along the I-90 corridor in Wisconsin. The \nlocation of the highest rainfall amounts will likely change as \nshown by the 00Z CAM suite where the location of heaviest \nrainfall may fall along the I-90 corridor in Wisconsin, or does \nit set up more in GRB or MKX's forecast area. \n\nConditions for the mid-morning to early afternoon should remain \nmostly dry as the boundary from the morning continues to shift south \nthrough the CWA. All CAMs have this boundary roughly through the \nmiddle of our forecast area during the early afternoon. Daytime \nrecovery will be ongoing as instability builds back in, giving the \narea anywhere from 3000 to 4500 J/kg of MLCAPE to work with. Given \nthat there is a boundary, however would like to see better surface \nconvergence along it for more widespread development, storms may \ninitiate along this boundary during the afternoon and early evening \nas it progresses southward. Compared to Thursday, there will be \nlittle to no shear (10 to 25 kts of effective shear) available so \nstorms will struggle to get going. For the storms that can get going \nand stick around for a little while, gusty winds and hail up to 1\" \nwould be the main threats with any strong to severe storm that \ndevelops. \n\nSaturday - Early Next Week: Continued Shower and Storm Chances with \nSlightly Above Normal Temperatures\n\nBy the weekend, ridging builds into the central US with the main \nridge axis setting up to our west. Our region remains in northwest \nflow aloft with various waves moving along the northern part of the \nridge giving us a continued chance (15 to 40%) of seeing showers and \nstorms for Sunday and Monday. There is better agreement within the \nlonger range models depicting a shortwave trough moving through the \nUpper Midwest Monday night into Tuesday. NBM PoPs for that timeframe \nare 40 to 75%. Temperatures cool down slightly from what we have \nseen this week, but remain slightly above normal with highs in the \nupper 70s to low 80s for Sunday and Monday with a brief warmup for \nmidweek with highs back into mid 80s.",
